<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">OGPU</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Joint State Political Directorate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The OGPU (Joint State Political Directorate) was the Soviet political police from 1923 to 1934, successor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and GPU. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-dzerzhinsky">Felix Dzerzhinsky</a> and then Vyacheslav Menzhinsky, it suppressed opposition, staged early show trials and enforced the collectivisation of agriculture, deporting some two million peasants as kulaks to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-settlements-in-the-soviet-union">special settlements</a> and expanding the system of forced-labour camps. In 1934 it was merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ogpu.jpg" alt="OGPU" width="176" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GPU_15th_anniversary_emblem.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was formed in December 1922, the ruling Russian Communist Party (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>) wanted a unified service to control state security across the new union. The State Political Directorate (GPU), successor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>, served only the Russian Socialist Federative Soviet Republic. On 15 November 1923 the GPU was dissolved and reformed as the Joint State Political Directorate, with jurisdiction over the whole Soviet Union and responsible to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a>. Felix Dzerzhinsky, who had chaired the GPU and the Cheka, was its first chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In theory the OGPU was to act with more restraint than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>, which had run the Red Terror from 1918 to 1922: it could not shoot counter-revolutionaries at will, and most suspects had to be brought before a judge. Its powers grew greatly in 1926, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/article-58">Article 58</a>, on anti-state terrorism, was added to the criminal code; its provisions were vaguely written and broadly interpreted. The Trust Operation of 1924-1925 drew White émigrés and foreign intelligence agencies into funding a fictitious anti-communist group, and led to the arrest and execution of Sidney Reilly after he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in September 1925.</p><p>Dzerzhinsky died in 1926 and was succeeded by his deputy Vyacheslav Menzhinsky. In March 1928 the Shakhty Trial prosecuted supposed industrial saboteurs, the first of many trials of Stalin's rule. The OGPU planned and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> system and carried out a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>, Greek Catholics, Roman Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and other religious bodies, headed by Yevgeny Tuchkov. During the First Five-Year Plan it enforced dekulakization through special troikas of OGPU agents, party officials and state procurators, who could sentence suspects to exile or death without a formal trial.</p><p>Menzhinsky's health deteriorated, and his first deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a> essentially took over as head in the late 1920s. Menzhinsky died in May 1934. In July 1934 the OGPU was dissolved and became the Main Directorate of State Security within the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs</a> (NKVD), under Yagoda. It was later transformed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kgb">Committee for State Security</a> (KGB) in 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joint State Political Directorate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The political police, successively the Cheka, <b>OGPU</b>, NKVD and from 1954 the KGB, enforced conformity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>OGPU</b> told the deported that they would regain their rights in five years.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
